Predicting Movie Rental Durations
A machine learning project built to help a DVD rental company predict how many days a customer will rent a movie, optimizing inventory planning and operational forecasting.

📌 Project Overview
DVD rental companies need to forecast customer rental durations efficiently to manage inventory, stock availability, and late fees. Using historical rental logs (rental_info.csv), this project applies data pre-processing, feature engineering, and regression modeling to predict the exact number of a customer's rental days (rental_length_days).

The primary business goal is to train a model that achieves a Mean Squared Error (MSE) of less than 3.0 on an unseen test dataset.

📂 Dataset Features
The dataset contains several key features regarding the movie, pricing, and ratings:

rental_date: The timestamp when the customer rented the DVD.

return_date: The timestamp when the customer returned the DVD.

amount & amount_2: The rental fee and its squared value.

rental_rate & rental_rate_2: The standard rate of the DVD and its squared value.

release_year: The year the movie was released.

length & length_2: The duration of the movie in minutes and its squared value.

replacement_cost: The cost to replace the DVD if lost or damaged.

special_features: Categorical tags (e.g., Deleted Scenes, Behind the Scenes).

Rating Dummies (NC-17, PG, PG-13, R): Binary variables indicating the film's rating (with reference categories dropped).

